Let $b_E$ be the number of games won by the boys. Then the number of games won by the girls was $\frac75b_E$.

Also, $3n-1$ games were won by the girls and $3(2n)-1-b_E=\frac{12n-1-b_E}2$ games were won by the boys.

We can set these two equal to each other to get $\frac{12n-1-b_E}2=\frac75 b_E$. Cross-multiplying now gives $5(12n-1-b_E)=14b_E$, or $60n - 5 - 5b_E = 14b_E$. Collecting terms gives $19b_E = 60n - 5$, Putting this through the divisibility test gives

\begin{align*}
60n-5 &\textrm{ divisible by 19.}
\end{align*}

Then $n\equiv\frac{10}3\bmod19$. Guessing, $n=20$ seems to work (it works, btw, the answer is correct), and $n\equiv\frac{10}3\bmod19$ is more in line with how divisibility works.

Let's try $n=16$, to make sure. We get

\begin{align*}
60\times16-5 &\textrm{ divisible by 19.}
\end{align*}

Therefore, the only value of $n$ which satisfies $n\equiv\frac{10}3\bmod19$ is $n=\boxed{20}$.

